1
00:00:00,600 --> 00:00:04,206
Welcome to the Modern Java Enterprise Handbook.

2
00:00:04,206 --> 00:00:08,963
This is chapter thirty-one: virtual threads, final in Java 21.

3
00:00:09,413 --> 00:00:16,642
Virtual threads let you keep writing plain blocking code, while the thread doing the blocking costs almost nothing.

4
00:00:16,642 --> 00:00:21,231
But they change what's scarce in your system, and that catches teams out.

5
00:00:22,131 --> 00:00:26,758
A dispatcher calls a vendor API that takes two hundred milliseconds.

6
00:00:26,758 --> 00:00:36,013
A pool of two hundred platform threads already reserves around two hundred megabytes of stack, and caps throughput at two hundred calls.

7
00:00:36,013 --> 00:00:41,865
Each thread spends almost all its time parked, holding an OS thread that does nothing.

8
00:00:42,315 --> 00:00:44,913
Before Java 21, you had two choices.

9
00:00:44,913 --> 00:00:52,420
Go reactive, which scales but costs you readable stack traces, debugging, and plain loops and try-catch.

10
00:00:52,420 --> 00:00:55,596
Or accept the ceiling and buy more machines.

11
00:00:55,596 --> 00:00:58,123
Virtual threads remove the dilemma.

12
00:00:59,023 --> 00:01:03,079
A platform thread maps one-to-one onto an OS thread.

13
00:01:03,079 --> 00:01:10,334
A virtual thread is a Java object, scheduled by the JVM onto a small pool of carrier threads.

14
00:01:10,334 --> 00:01:12,207
Millions onto a handful.

15
00:01:12,657 --> 00:01:15,021
The key operation is unmounting.

16
00:01:15,021 --> 00:01:23,665
When a virtual thread blocks on something the JDK understands, like a socket read, sleep, a lock or a blocking queue,

17
00:01:24,115 --> 00:01:29,533
its stack is copied to the heap, and the carrier is released to run another virtual thread.

18
00:01:29,983 --> 00:01:37,215
When the data arrives, the stack is copied back onto some carrier, and execution resumes on the very next line.

19
00:01:37,665 --> 00:01:40,876
From the code's point of view, nothing happened.

20
00:01:40,876 --> 00:01:43,217
The read just blocked and returned.

21
00:01:43,217 --> 00:01:47,564
That's the whole design goal: blocking syntax, non-blocking cost.

22
00:01:48,464 --> 00:01:49,833
Here's what changes.

23
00:01:49,833 --> 00:01:54,900
A platform thread is an OS thread with about a megabyte of reserved stack.

24
00:01:54,900 --> 00:01:59,966
A virtual thread is a heap object whose stack grows and shrinks as needed.

25
00:01:59,966 --> 00:02:05,375
Creating one is sub-microsecond, so you can have millions instead of thousands.

26
00:02:05,375 --> 00:02:10,099
Pooling, essential for platform threads, is harmful for virtual ones.

27
00:02:10,099 --> 00:02:13,317
And for CPU-bound work, nothing changes at all.

28
00:02:14,217 --> 00:02:17,321
Here's the dispatcher with virtual threads.

29
00:02:17,321 --> 00:02:21,434
newVirtualThreadPerTaskExecutor, in a try-with-resources.

30
00:02:21,434 --> 00:02:26,197
Ten thousand orders means ten thousand concurrent virtual threads.

31
00:02:26,197 --> 00:02:28,795
The blocking send call is unchanged.

32
00:02:28,795 --> 00:02:33,631
And close waits for every task, so the method doesn't return early.

33
00:02:34,081 --> 00:02:36,110
That executor isn't a pool.

34
00:02:36,110 --> 00:02:40,319
It creates a virtual thread per task and throws it away.

35
00:02:40,319 --> 00:02:43,701
Same code, same stack traces, same try-catch.

36
00:02:43,701 --> 00:02:47,158
The waiting just no longer holds an OS thread.

37
00:02:48,058 --> 00:02:49,825
Mistake one: pooling them.

38
00:02:49,825 --> 00:02:57,302
A fixed pool of two hundred virtual threads caps concurrency at two hundred, whatever the threads are made of.

39
00:02:57,302 --> 00:03:02,671
Pooling exists because platform threads are expensive, and virtual ones aren't.

40
00:03:03,121 --> 00:03:05,467
Pool the scarce resource instead.

41
00:03:05,467 --> 00:03:10,585
If the vendor tolerates fifty concurrent calls, say so with a semaphore.

42
00:03:10,585 --> 00:03:18,545
Ten thousand virtual threads exist, fifty are in the call, and the rest wait on the semaphore at almost no cost.

43
00:03:19,445 --> 00:03:22,179
Mistake two: expecting a linear speedup.

44
00:03:22,179 --> 00:03:27,372
Virtual threads remove the thread ceiling and expose whatever was behind it.

45
00:03:27,372 --> 00:03:31,062
A connection pool of twenty still runs twenty queries.

46
00:03:31,062 --> 00:03:33,590
The other threads just queue cheaply.

47
00:03:33,590 --> 00:03:37,280
Throughput is bounded by your slowest shared resource.

48
00:03:37,730 --> 00:03:39,512
Mistake three: ThreadLocal.

49
00:03:39,512 --> 00:03:45,453
An eight-kilobyte buffer per thread was one and a half megabytes with two hundred threads.

50
00:03:45,453 --> 00:03:48,886
With a million virtual threads it's eight gigabytes.

51
00:03:48,886 --> 00:03:55,223
ThreadLocal still works, but audit anything heavy, and prefer scoped values for request context.

52
00:03:55,673 --> 00:03:57,853
Mistake four: CPU-bound work.

53
00:03:57,853 --> 00:04:01,535
Virtual threads multiplex waiting, not computing.

54
00:04:01,535 --> 00:04:06,420
Ten thousand of them doing arithmetic still share the same cores.

55
00:04:06,420 --> 00:04:09,126
Ask: is the task waiting or working?

56
00:04:09,126 --> 00:04:11,831
Waiting scales with virtual threads.

57
00:04:11,831 --> 00:04:13,785
Working scales with cores.

58
00:04:14,685 --> 00:04:19,490
A pinned virtual thread blocks its carrier instead of unmounting.

59
00:04:19,490 --> 00:04:24,813
And with only a few carriers, a few pinned threads can stall everything.

60
00:04:24,813 --> 00:04:29,618
On Java 21 to 23, the big cause was blocking inside synchronized.

61
00:04:29,618 --> 00:04:36,936
Java 24 fixed that, so the old advice to replace synchronized with ReentrantLock is obsolete there.

62
00:04:37,386 --> 00:04:39,605
Object dot wait was fixed too.

63
00:04:40,055 --> 00:04:42,956
Native frames from JNI calls still pin.

64
00:04:43,406 --> 00:04:46,393
And so does blocking during class initialisation.

65
00:04:46,843 --> 00:04:52,923
To find pinning, record with Java Flight Recorder and print the VirtualThreadPinned events.

66
00:04:53,823 --> 00:05:03,462
Under the hood, virtual threads run on their own FIFO ForkJoinPool, separate from the common pool, with one carrier per core by default.

67
00:05:03,462 --> 00:05:06,580
The defaults are right for nearly every app.

68
00:05:07,030 --> 00:05:09,962
A virtual thread's stack lives on the heap.

69
00:05:09,962 --> 00:05:15,213
Blocking freezes live frames into a heap chunk, and resuming thaws them back.

70
00:05:15,213 --> 00:05:20,531
So shallow tasks cost a few hundred bytes, and stacks are garbage-collectable.

71
00:05:20,531 --> 00:05:25,441
But deep recursion in a task that blocks often costs more than it looks.

72
00:05:25,891 --> 00:05:28,887
And jstack doesn't show virtual threads.

73
00:05:28,887 --> 00:05:38,627
Use jcmd Thread dot dump to file, in JSON format, which also groups threads by the structured concurrency scope that created them.

74
00:05:39,527 --> 00:05:40,698
Production rules.

75
00:05:40,698 --> 00:05:44,005
Use the per-task executor in try-with-resources.

76
00:05:44,005 --> 00:05:45,796
Write plain blocking code.

77
00:05:45,796 --> 00:05:48,759
Limit the scarce resource with a semaphore.

78
00:05:48,759 --> 00:05:52,204
Audit heavy ThreadLocals and prefer scoped values.

79
00:05:52,204 --> 00:05:54,822
Keep CPU-bound work on platform pools.

80
00:05:54,822 --> 00:06:01,436
And review your connection pools and downstream limits, because they become the visible ceiling.

81
00:06:01,886 --> 00:06:03,835
Never pool virtual threads.

82
00:06:03,835 --> 00:06:07,515
Never use ThreadLocal as a cache for large objects.

83
00:06:07,515 --> 00:06:12,712
Never assume a throughput win without checking what actually limits you.

84
00:06:12,712 --> 00:06:15,671
Never use them for CPU-bound parallelism.

85
00:06:15,671 --> 00:06:22,238
And don't carry Java 21 pinning workarounds into Java 24 without knowing why they're there.

86
00:06:23,138 --> 00:06:23,756
To recap.

87
00:06:23,756 --> 00:06:31,167
Virtual threads give you blocking code at non-blocking cost, by moving the stack to the heap while it waits.

88
00:06:31,167 --> 00:06:36,108
Never pool them, and limit the scarce resource with a semaphore instead.

89
00:06:36,108 --> 00:06:39,882
They expose your real bottleneck, usually the database.

90
00:06:39,882 --> 00:06:42,215
Waiting scales, computing doesn't.

91
00:06:42,215 --> 00:06:45,303
And synchronized pinning is fixed in Java 24.

92
00:06:45,753 --> 00:06:48,987
Next is chapter thirty-two: structured concurrency.

93
00:06:48,987 --> 00:06:50,255
Thanks for watching.
